---
/**
 * Fender Precision Bass dating guide. SIXTH OF THE MODEL-AXIS SPOKES.
 *
 * Sibling of the Stratocaster, Telecaster, Jazzmaster, Jaguar and Jazz Bass
 * dating guides. Built on joe/precision-bass-dating-spoke, stacked on
 * joe/jazz-bass-dating-spoke.
 *
 * WHAT MAKES THE PRECISION BASS PAGE DIFFERENT. Do not clone the other five:
 *   - THIS MODEL IS THREE DIFFERENT INSTRUMENTS WEARING ONE NAME, and that is
 *     the hook. A 1952 slab-body single-coil bass with a Telecaster headstock
 *     shares almost nothing visually with a 1959 gold guard. No other Fender
 *     was rebuilt this fundamentally mid-life. So the FIRST question on this
 *     page is not "what year" but "WHICH Precision Bass am I holding", and the
 *     era section comes before the five fastest checks.
 *   - IT IS ALSO DEFINED BY WHAT IT DIDN'T GET. No fingerboard binding, no
 *     block inlays, no three-bolt neck, no bullet truss rod, ever, in standard
 *     production. That is a genuinely useful authentication angle (blocks or a
 *     three-bolt plate on a "vintage Precision" is a red flag) and it is the
 *     mirror image of the Jazz Bass page, which has all of those changes.
 *   - The bass-side thumb rest, the tug bar, the ashtray covers and the foam
 *     mute bonded inside the bridge cover are all Precision ground.
 *
 * JOE'S REVIEW RULES, carried over from the five sibling pages:
 *   - Features give a WINDOW. Only the neck heel date and the body date give a
 *     month.
 *   - The serial belongs in the second pass, read as one vote among several.
 *   - Never call a feature "model-specific" without checking it.
 *   - Never say "bare wood" about a body date.
 *   - Keep a photo caption on the one thing the photo is for.
 *
 * FACT SOURCING. Every dated claim traces to the Precision Bass fact set added
 * to joes-vintage-databases as VG-0131 to VG-0146 (PR #5):
 *   VG-0131 launch autumn 1951        VG-0139 gold-guard hardware set
 *   VG-0132 slab-era screws + patent  VG-0140 clay to pearloid late 1964
 *   VG-0133 CONTOURS MID-1954         VG-0141 NEVER got binding or blocks
 *   VG-0134 mid-1957 rebuild          VG-0142 tuners (F stamp vs Schaller)
 *   VG-0135 gold-guard neck profiles  VG-0143 pickguards + finish pairing
 *   VG-0136 1958 3-tone + "unburst"   VG-0144 FOUR-BOLT throughout the 1970s
 *   VG-0137 mid-1959 rosewood + tort  VG-0145 logo: SILVER spaghetti
 *   VG-0138 anodized guard wear tell  VG-0146 pickup bottoms March 1964
 *
 * LOGO COLOUR: SILVER on this model, and silver through BOTH the spaghetti
 * AND the transition eras. Joe ruled silver 2026-08-10, correcting his own
 * earlier answer in the same session which had said gold, then extended it
 * 2026-08-13: the transition logo here stays silver, so the 1964 change is a
 * change in the WEIGHT of the script, not the colour, and only the black CBS
 * logo from late 1967 changes colour at all. (That mirrors the Strat and
 * Jazzmaster problem in reverse: there both eras are GOLD, here both are
 * SILVER, and on neither model does colour separate spaghetti from
 * transition.) The full spaghetti set is Stratocaster GOLD, Jazzmaster GOLD,
 * Telecaster SILVER on the standard and GOLD on the Custom, Precision Bass
 * SILVER. The Jaguar and the Jazz Bass never wore a spaghetti logo at all.
 * Do NOT harmonise these. VG-0145 records only the spaghetti half and needs
 * the transition-era extension in the next joes-vintage-databases PR.
 *
 * TWO OF JOE'S ANSWERS WERE DOUBTED IN FACT-CHECKING AND BOTH HELD UP, so do
 * not "correct" them back: the Precision never took binding or blocks
 * (VG-0141; Fender's MODERN "seventies Precision" reissue is bound and blocked
 * but borrows Jazz Bass styling and is not a vintage spec), and it kept the
 * four-bolt neck and heel truss rod through the 1970s (VG-0144; the Vintage
 * Guitar and Bass spec page for an actual 1972 Precision says four bolts).
 *
 * ONE CORRECTION, VG-0142 carries verifyWithExpert: the F-stamped tuners are
 * Fender in-house from late 1968 to early 1969, and Schaller only took over
 * manufacture by 1976. Joe's answer had attributed the 1968 tuners to Schaller.
 *
 * CONTRADICTION IN THE SOURCE, flagged to Joe and NOT propagated: the live
 * gold-guard post opens with the slab body giving way to "a contoured body and
 * a hum-canceling split-coil pickup" in 1957, which reads as though contouring
 * arrived with that redesign. The 1952 guide argues specifically for MID-1954
 * and warns that a 1953 with true contours is not a 1953. This page follows the
 * 1952 guide.
 *
 * MODEL CODE: the neck-dates table lists 5 for the Precision Bass with NO era
 * qualifier, unlike the Stratocaster (2 to 13 to 22) and Jaguar (1 to 15) rows
 * which record their renumbering. So this page states code 5 and says the table
 * records no second code, rather than claiming it never moved.
 *
 * PHOTO NOTE: coverage is excellent for 1952/53 and 1959 and almost nonexistent
 * for 1954 to 1956 and after 1960. Those eras are text-only on purpose.
 *
 * NO YEAR-TO-SERIAL-NUMBER TABLE, same as the five sibling pages.
 */
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import LicensedImage from "../components/primitives/LicensedImage.astro";
import { resolveImage, assetSrc, ogCardSize } from "../lib/images";
import { contact, hrefs, SITE_URL } from "../config/site";

const meta = (file: string) => resolveImage("images/fender-sn/" + file)!;
const bmeta = (slug: string, file: string) => resolveImage(`images/blog/${slug}/${file}`)!;

const PB52 = "1952-fender-precision-bass-guide";
const PBGG = "gold-guard-fender-precision-bass";

const PAGE_PATH = "/fender-precision-bass-dating-guide/";
const HUB_PATH = "/fender-guitars-serial-number-guide/";
const HUB_HREF = HUB_PATH;
const OG = `images/blog/${PBGG}/1959-fender-precision-bass-full-shot-scaled.jpg`;

// ---- Data ----
// The three designs. This is the page's spine and it comes before the year table.
const eraRows: [string, string, string][] = [
  [
    "The slab bass",
    "Late 1951 to mid-1954",
    "A flat slab ash body with no contouring at all, a single-coil pickup, and a Telecaster-shaped headstock. Blonde nitrocellulose, slotted screws early on, and the serial number stamped into the bridge plate. Visually it has almost nothing in common with a modern Precision.",
  ],
  [
    "The contoured single coil",
    "Mid-1954 to mid-1957",
    "The Stratocaster-style forearm bevel and belly cut arrive, so the body becomes the shape people recognise, but the pickup is still the original single coil and the headstock is still the Telecaster shape. The shortest of the three eras and the least photographed.",
  ],
  [
    "The split coil",
    "Mid-1957 onward",
    "The rebuild. A hum-cancelling split-coil pickup, a gold anodized aluminum pickguard, and the larger headstock. From here the instrument is recognisably the Precision Bass that everything since has copied.",
  ],
];

const yearRows: [string, string, string][] = [
  [
    "1951",
    "Launched in the autumn as the first solidbody electric bass to really catch on. <a class='il' href='#slab'>Slab ash body</a> with no contours, <a class='il' href='#pickups'>single-coil pickup</a>, Telecaster-shaped headstock, blonde nitrocellulose finish. Hardware is entirely slotted-head and the serial number is stamped into the <a class='il' href='#serial'>bridge plate</a>.",
    "A flat slab body with no forearm or belly cut anywhere on it.",
  ],
  [
    "1952",
    "The most discussed year of the <a class='il' href='#slab'>slab era</a>. Around the middle of the year Phillips-head screws start replacing slotted ones in some hardware positions. Serial ranges expand considerably. Otherwise the specification holds.",
    "Mixed slotted and Phillips screws. Both are correct on this year.",
  ],
  [
    "1953",
    "Production settles. Phillips screws are more uniform. Some examples show noticeably softer, more rounded perimeter edges from heavier hand sanding, which is not the same thing as contouring. The <a class='il' href='#serial'>Patent Pending</a> wording begins changing to an actual patent number on some late examples.",
    "Softer edges, but still no true forearm bevel or belly cut.",
  ],
  [
    "1954",
    "Around the middle of the year the Stratocaster-style <a class='il' href='#slab'>contours</a> arrive, the deep forearm bevel and the rear belly cut. This is the cleanest single dividing line in the model's early history.",
    "True contours. If it has them it is mid-1954 or later, whatever the seller says.",
  ],
  [
    "1955 to 1956",
    "The quiet stretch. A contoured body carrying the original <a class='il' href='#pickups'>single-coil pickup</a> and the Telecaster-shaped <a class='il' href='#headstock'>headstock</a>. The least documented and least photographed era of the model.",
    "Contours plus a single coil plus a Tele headstock, all at once.",
  ],
  [
    "1957",
    "The rebuild lands around the middle of the year: the hum-cancelling <a class='il' href='#pickups'>split-coil pickup</a>, the <a class='il' href='#goldguard'>gold anodized aluminum pickguard</a>, and the new larger <a class='il' href='#headstock'>headstock</a>. A handful of genuine transitional basses carry the new pickup with the older Telecaster headstock still fitted. The neck profile is a soft V carried over from the slab era.",
    "A split pickup in two halves rather than one bar, under a gold metal guard.",
  ],
  [
    "1958",
    "Red is added to the sunburst between the black and the yellow, making it three-tone. That red was highly sensitive to ultraviolet light and often faded away completely, which is why so many now read as two-tone. Collectors call the faded result an unburst. The <a class='il' href='#neck'>neck</a> moves to a very thick, wide C.",
    "A very chunky neck, and a sunburst that may have lost its red entirely.",
  ],
  [
    "1959",
    "Around the middle of the year the rosewood slab <a class='il' href='#neck'>fingerboard</a> and the four ply tortoiseshell celluloid pickguard arrive, <a class='il' href='#gg-end'>ending the gold anodized guard</a> and the maple-neck gold-guard era.",
    "Rosewood instead of maple, and tortoiseshell instead of gold metal.",
  ],
  [
    "1960 to 1963",
    "The specification holds. Rosewood board with clay dots, tortoiseshell guard, split-coil pickup, reverse-wind Kluson tuners.",
    "Nothing visual to separate these years. The <a class='il' href='#heel'>heel stamp</a> and the <a class='il' href='/fender-pot-codes/'>pots</a> do the work.",
  ],
  [
    "1964",
    "Two changes, months apart. In March the pickup <a class='il' href='#pickups'>bobbin flatwork</a> moves from black to grey. Late in the year the clay <a class='il' href='#neck'>dots</a> give way to pearloid, and the <a class='il' href='#headstock'>logo</a> moves from the thin spaghetti script to the heavier transition decal, staying silver through the change.",
    "Pull the pickup for the bottom colour, then look at the weight of the logo.",
  ],
  [
    "1965 to 1967",
    "CBS completes the purchase in January 1965. Early to mid 1965 the pickguard material moves from nitrate to vinyl. Early 1966 the reverse-wind <a class='il' href='#tuners'>Klusons</a> give way to non-reverse. The Precision does not follow the Jazz Bass into <a class='il' href='#neck'>binding or block inlays</a> at any point.",
    "Still an unbound board with dots, which on this model is correct all the way through.",
  ],
  [
    "1968 to 1971",
    "The bold black CBS <a class='il' href='#headstock'>logo</a> arrives from late 1967 into 1968, and the F-stamped <a class='il' href='#tuners'>tuners</a> follow from late 1968 into early 1969.",
    "A black logo and a large stylized F on the tuner back plates.",
  ],
  [
    "1972 to 1975",
    "The Precision keeps its <a class='il' href='#seventies'>four-bolt neck plate</a> and its heel-adjusted truss rod while the Stratocaster and the Jazz Bass move to three-bolt Micro-Tilt necks with bullet truss rods. Tuner manufacture moves to Schaller by 1976.",
    "Count the neck bolts. Four is correct here, and three is a problem.",
  ],
];

// The checks that place a Precision Bass without tools. These give a WINDOW.
const fastRows: [string, string, string, string][] = [
  ["The body", "Slab to mid-1954, contoured after", "Look for the forearm bevel and the belly cut. A flat slab is 1951 to mid-1954, and it is the most valuable version of the bass.", "#slab"],
  ["The pickup", "Single coil to mid-1957, split coil after", "One bar under the cover or two offset halves. After the body shape, this is the check that decides the era.", "#pickups"],
  ["The pickguard", "Gold anodized aluminum 1957 to mid-1959, tortoiseshell after", "Metal or plastic, and you can tell across a room. The gold guard window is under two years.", "#goldguard"],
  ["The fingerboard", "Maple to mid-1959, rosewood after, clay dots to late 1964 then pearloid", "Rosewood arrives with the tortoiseshell guard, so those two travel together.", "#neck"],
  ["The logo", "Silver spaghetti to 1964, silver transition to 1967, black CBS after", "Silver on this model where a Stratocaster is gold, and it stays silver through the transition era, so read the weight of the script rather than the colour.", "#headstock"],
];

// Serial locations. Named, NOT charted. Charts live on the hub.
const plateRows: [string, string, string][] = [
  ["Bridge plate", "1951 to about 1954", "Stamped into the steel bridge plate rather than a neck plate, alongside the Patent Pending marking on early examples."],
  ["Plain neck plate", "About 1954 to late 1962", "Four or five digits on a plain chrome four-bolt plate, no letter."],
  ["L-prefix plate", "Late 1962 to late 1965", "An L in front of five digits. The last of the pre-CBS plates."],
  ["Big-F plate", "About late 1965 to 1976", "Six digits stamped above a large stylized F. Still a four-bolt plate on this model."],
];

const tocItems: [string, string][] = [
  ["#start", "Start Here"],
  ["#eras", "The Three Precision Basses"],
  ["#fast", "The Five Fastest Checks"],
  ["#years", "Precision Bass Year By Year"],
  ["#slab", "The Slab Years"],
  ["#goldguard", "The Gold Guard Window"],
  ["#headstock", "The Logo"],
  ["#neck", "Board, Dots And What It Never Got"],
  ["#pickups", "Single Coil To Split Coil"],
  ["#tuners", "Tuners"],
  ["#heel", "The Neck Heel Date"],
  ["#body", "Body Dates"],
  ["#serial", "What The Serial Number Adds"],
  ["#seventies", "What The Precision Skipped"],
  ["#limits", "What Features Can't Prove"],
  ["#cross", "Confirm It From The Inside"],
  ["#faq", "Common Questions"],
  ["#appraisal", "Free Expert Appraisal"],
  ["#about-joe", "About Joe"],
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "What Year Is My Precision Bass?",
    a: "Start with a different question than you would on any other Fender: which Precision Bass is it. Three quite different instruments wear the name. A flat slab body with a single-coil pickup and a Telecaster-shaped headstock is late 1951 to mid-1954. A contoured body still carrying that single coil is mid-1954 to mid-1957. A split-coil pickup is mid-1957 or later. Once you know which one you are holding, the pickguard, the fingerboard and the logo narrow it further, and then the neck heel date, the body date and the pot codes give you a month.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is A Slab Body Precision Bass Really Worth That Much More?",
    a: "Yes, and the reason is scarcity plus the fact that it is the first one. The slab era ran from late 1951 to mid-1954, so it is under three years of production of an instrument that has been made continuously ever since. Be careful with 1953 though: some 1953 basses have noticeably softer, more rounded perimeter edges from heavier hand sanding on the line, and that gets described as early contouring by people who want it to be. It is not. The true Stratocaster-style forearm bevel and belly cut did not reach the Precision until mid-1954, so a bass with real contours is not a 1953 no matter what the listing says.",
  },
  {
    q: "What Is A Gold Guard Precision Bass?",
    a: "The version Fender built between the mid-1957 rebuild and mid-1959: a split-coil pickup under a gold anodized aluminum pickguard, on a maple neck. The window is under two years, which is what makes them collectable. The metal guard was not decoration either, it shielded the electronics against hum and radio interference and it stayed flat where the earlier single-ply plastic guards warped and cracked. Look for the gold anodizing worn through to bare silver aluminum where a player's thumb rested. That patch takes decades to develop and is very hard to fake convincingly.",
  },
  {
    q: "My Precision Bass Sunburst Looks Two Tone. Has It Been Refinished?",
    a: "Probably not. Fender added red to the sunburst in 1958, between the black and the yellow, and the red dye of that era was highly sensitive to ultraviolet light. On plenty of examples it has faded out completely, leaving what looks like a two-tone burst. Collectors call that an unburst and it is a normal ageing outcome rather than evidence of a respray. What you want to check instead is whether the finish behaves consistently everywhere, and whether anything is sitting over the body date rather than under it.",
  },
  {
    q: "Did The Precision Bass Ever Have Block Inlays Or A Bound Fingerboard?",
    a: "Not in standard production, at any point in the vintage era. That is Jazz Bass territory, where binding arrived in late 1965 and block inlays in mid-1966. The Precision stayed an unbound, dot-inlay instrument throughout. The confusion is understandable, because Fender's modern seventies Precision Bass reissues are sold with bound, block-inlay necks, but those borrow Jazz Bass styling rather than recreating a vintage Precision specification. So blocks and binding on something being sold to you as an original vintage Precision need a very good explanation, and rare custom-order exceptions aside, the honest answer is usually that the neck came from somewhere else.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does My 1970s Precision Bass Have A Three Bolt Neck?",
    a: "It should not. This is one of the more useful differences between the two basses: the Stratocaster and the Jazz Bass moved to the three-bolt Micro-Tilt neck joint with a bullet truss rod, the Jazz Bass in late 1974, but the Precision Bass kept its four-bolt neck plate and its heel-adjusted truss rod right through the 1970s. So a three-bolt plate on a bass presented as a vintage Precision is worth a hard look, and so is a bullet adjuster at the headstock.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is The Spaghetti Logo On A Precision Bass Gold Or Silver?",
    a: "Silver on this model. It differs from a Stratocaster, where the spaghetti logo is gold, and getting it backwards is a common way to misread a headstock. The full picture across the line: gold on the Stratocaster and the Jazzmaster, silver on the standard Telecaster with gold on the Custom, and silver on the Precision Bass. The silver does not stop with the spaghetti script either, because the transition logo that follows it in 1964 is silver as well, so on a Precision Bass that change is one of weight rather than colour, thin and fine giving way to the same silver drawn much heavier. The black CBS logo from late 1967 is the only colour change on the headstock. The Jaguar and the Jazz Bass never wore a spaghetti logo at all, because both arrived after that era had ended.",
  },
  {
    q: "Should I Take My Precision Bass Apart To Date It?",
    a: "Only as far as you need to. Everything in the first two passes reads with the bass assembled, which is deliberate. Pulling the pickup to read the bottom colour is worth it on a bass from either side of March 1964, and pulling the control plate for pot codes is standard. Be careful with an anodized gold guard, which marks if a screwdriver slips, and with old nitrate guards, which crack at the screw ears. If the outside checks leave you stuck between two years, send me photos before you reach for a tool.",
  },
];

// ---- Schema ----
const articleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  headline: "How To Date A Fender Precision Bass, Year By Year",
  description:
    "Date a vintage Fender Precision Bass from what you can see: slab or contoured body, single coil or split coil, the gold anodized guard, and the logo, from the 1951 launch to 1975.",
  author: { "@id": `${SITE_URL}/about-me/#person` },
  publisher: { "@id": `${SITE_URL}/` },
  image: `${SITE_URL}${assetSrc(OG)}`,
  datePublished: "2026-08-10",
  dateModified: "2026-08-10",
  inLanguage: "en-US",
