---

<Layout
  title="How To Date A Fender Precision Bass: Year By Year Guide"
  description="Date a vintage Fender Precision Bass from what you can see: slab or contoured body, single coil or split coil, the gold anodized guard, and the logo, 1951 to 1975."
  canonical={PAGE_PATH}
  ogImage={`${SITE_URL}${assetSrc(OG)}`}
  {...ogCardSize(OG)}
  ogImageAlt="A 1959 Fender Precision Bass with its gold anodized aluminum pickguard and maple fingerboard"
  ogType="article"
  noIndex={false}
  structuredData={[articleSchema, faqSchema, breadcrumbSchema]}
>
  <!-- Archetype B header backdrop: solid bands the absolute site Header sits on -->
  <div class="fspot-header-backdrop" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="fspot-header-backdrop__dark"></div>
    <div class="fspot-header-backdrop__cream"></div>
  </div>

  <div class="fspot">
    <!-- HERO -->
    <section class="hero" id="top">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <a class="back" href={HUB_HREF}>Back To The Full Fender Serial Guide</a>
        <span class="eyebrow">Fender Dating By Model</span>
        <h1>How To Date A Fender Precision Bass</h1>
        <p class="lead">Ask which one before you ask which year. Three genuinely different instruments wear this name, and a 1952 slab bass with a single-coil pickup and a Telecaster headstock shares almost nothing visually with the 1959 below. No other Fender was rebuilt this thoroughly halfway through its own life. Sort out which of the three you are holding and the year follows quickly. Here is what changed and when, from the autumn 1951 launch through 1975.</p>
        <div class="trust">
          <span>Joe Dampt</span><span>12 years buying vintage Fenders full-time</span><span>10,000+ appraised</span>
        </div>
        <div class="hubcta">
          <p class="hubcta__t"><b>Just after the serial number?</b> The full charts and the lookup tool that decodes a number for you are on the main guide.</p>
          <a class="hubcta__b" href={HUB_HREF}>Look Up A Serial Number</a>
        </div>
        <figure class="sfig herofig">
          <LicensedImage src={bmeta(PBGG, "1959-fender-precision-bass-full-shot-scaled.jpg")} alt="Full body shot of a 1959 Fender Precision Bass with a gold anodized aluminum pickguard and a maple fingerboard" width={1400} loading="eager" />
          <figcaption>A 1959 Precision Bass in the gold guard specification. Split-coil pickup, gold anodized aluminum guard, maple neck: a window of under two years.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- START HERE -->
    <section id="start">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Start Here</span>
        <h2>Read The Bass In Three Passes</h2>
        <p>The Precision Bass runs differently from the other model pages on this site, because the first question is not a year at all. Establish which of the three designs you have, then narrow inside it.</p>
        <div class="passes">
          <div class="pass">
            <span class="pass__n">Pass One</span>
            <h3>Across The Room</h3>
            <p>The body shape and the pickup. Slab or contoured, one coil or two offset halves. Those two questions place the bass in one of three eras spanning twenty-four years.</p>
            <div class="pass__go">
              <a href="#eras">The three eras</a>
              <a href="#slab">The body</a>
              <a href="#pickups">The pickup</a>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="pass">
            <span class="pass__n">Pass Two</span>
            <h3>In Your Hands</h3>
            <p>The pickguard material, the fingerboard, the logo, the tuners, and the serial number. Take the serial as one vote among several, and note that on the earliest basses it is not even on a neck plate.</p>
            <div class="pass__go">
              <a href="#goldguard">The pickguard</a>
              <a href="#neck">The fingerboard</a>
              <a href="#headstock">The logo</a>
              <a href="#tuners">The tuners</a>
              <a href="#serial">The serial number</a>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="pass">
            <span class="pass__n">Pass Three</span>
            <h3>Inside The Bass</h3>
            <p>The neck heel date, the body date, and the pot codes, plus a pickup pulled to read the bottom colour. These are the only places Fender wrote a real date.</p>
            <div class="pass__go">
              <a href="#heel">The neck heel date</a>
              <a href="#body">Body dates</a>
              <a href="#pickups">Pickup bottoms</a>
              <a href="/fender-pot-codes/">Pot codes</a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p><b>The three passes should agree.</b> When they don't, the disagreement is the interesting part. On this model it most often means a neck from another bass, or a body that has been refinished and lost its markings.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- SAFETY -->
    <section class="pt0" aria-label="Safety warning">
      <div class="measure">
        <div class="warnbox" role="note">
          <span class="warnbox__head"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M10.29 3.86 1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0Z"></path><path d="M12 9v4"></path><path d="M12 17h.01"></path></svg>Read This First</span>
          <p>Both of the first two passes read with the bass assembled. Two things to be careful with on this model if you do go in: a gold anodized guard marks permanently if a screwdriver slips off a screw head, and sixty-year-old nitrate guards crack at the screw ears. If the outside checks leave you stuck, contact me before you take anything apart.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- THE THREE ERAS: THE HOOK -->
    <section id="eras">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">The Precision Difference</span>
        <h2>The Three Precision Basses</h2>
        <p>Every other model page on this site narrows a year inside one design. Here you are choosing between three, because Fender rebuilt this instrument twice while keeping the name. Work out which one is in front of you first and everything afterwards gets easier.</p>
        <div class="tablewrap"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>Which one</th><th>Years</th><th>What you are looking at</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{eraRows.map((r) => (<tr><td data-label="Which one"><b>{r[0]}</b></td><td data-label="Years" class="yr">{r[1]}</td><td data-label="What you are looking at">{r[2]}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
        </table></div>
        <div class="tip">
          <p><b>The middle era is the one people forget exists.</b> Between mid-1954 and mid-1957 the Precision had a contoured body and the original single-coil pickup at the same time, under a Telecaster-shaped headstock. Sellers who only know the two famous versions tend to read a contoured body as "post-1957" and price it accordingly, or read a single coil as "slab era" and date it three years too early. If you have contours and a single coil together, you are in that window, and it is the least photographed stretch of the model's life.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- FIVE FASTEST -->
    <section id="fast">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">The Short Version</span>
        <h2>The Five Fastest Checks</h2>
        <p>If you read nothing else on this page, read these five. Read them together rather than one at a time: no single row narrows anything to a year, but five rows overlapping do.</p>
        <div class="tablewrap"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>What to look at</th><th>What it tells you</th><th>How to read it</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{fastRows.map((r) => (<tr><td data-label="What to look at"><a class="rowjump" href={r[3]}>{r[0]}</a></td><td data-label="What it tells you" class="yr">{r[1]}</td><td data-label="How to read it">{r[2]}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
        </table></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- YEAR BY YEAR -->
    <section id="years">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">The Whole Timeline</span>
        <h2>Precision Bass Year By Year, 1951 To 1975</h2>
        <p>What changed each year, and the quickest way to see it. Fender never made a clean cutover, so a change dated to mid-year means both versions turn up on instruments from that year and a transition example is genuinely correct either way. And parts sat in bins before they went into a bass, so a feature dates the part, and the instrument is that year or later.</p>
        <div class="tablewrap"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>What changed</th><th>Fastest tell</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{yearRows.map((r) => (<tr><td data-label="Year" class="yr">{r[0]}</td><td data-label="What changed" set:html={r[1]}></td><td data-label="Fastest tell" set:html={r[2]}></td></tr>))}</tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p class="note-sm">For the long version of the slab era, the <a class="spoke" href="/post/1952-fender-precision-bass-guide/">1952 Precision Bass guide</a> goes through it in detail, and the <a class="spoke" href="/post/gold-guard-fender-precision-bass/">gold guard guide</a> does the same for 1957 to 1959.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- SLAB -->
    <section id="slab">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">The First Design</span>
        <h2>The Slab Years, 1951 To Mid-1954</h2>
        <p>Under three years of production of an instrument that has been built continuously ever since, which is most of why these carry the money they do. A slab Precision is a flat ash plank with no forearm bevel and no belly cut, wearing a single-coil pickup and a Telecaster-shaped headstock.</p>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(PB52, "1952-fender-precision-bass-pickup-scaled.jpg")} alt="The original single-coil pickup on a 1952 Fender Precision Bass, showing white string wrapping over the coil windings" width={1400} />
            <figcaption>The single-coil pickup on a 1952. The white string wrapped over the coil windings is one of the quickest ways to recognise an original early pickup.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p>Inside the era, the useful marker is hardware. Late 1951 examples are entirely slotted-head. Phillips-head screws start appearing in some positions around the middle of 1952 and are more uniform by 1953, so a mix of the two on a 1952 is correct rather than evidence of later work. The Patent Pending wording on the decal and the bridge plate starts giving way to an actual patent number on some late 1953 basses.</p>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(PB52, "1952-fender-precision-bass-string-tree-scaled.jpg")} alt="Macro photograph of the round string retainer on the headstock of a 1952 Fender Precision Bass, held by a Phillips head screw" width={1600} />
            <figcaption>The string retainer on a 1952, held by a <b>Phillips</b> screw. Positions like this one are where the changeover shows, so a bass with both head types is reading correctly rather than wrongly.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <div class="tip">
          <p><b>Be careful with 1953 and the word contoured.</b> Some 1953 basses show noticeably softer, more rounded perimeter edges, the result of heavier hand sanding on the line as Fender began thinking about ergonomics ahead of the Stratocaster. That is edge softening. It is not contouring. The deep forearm bevel and the rear belly cut did not reach the Precision Bass until <b>mid-1954</b>, so a bass with true Stratocaster-style contours is not a 1953, whatever the listing says.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(PB52, "1953-fender-precision-bass-bridge-and-saddles-scaled.jpg")} alt="Bridge and saddles on a 1953 Fender Precision Bass showing pressed fiber barrel saddles, the serial number stamp, and the Patent Pending marking" width={1400} />
            <figcaption>The 1953 bridge, with the serial number stamped into the plate and the Patent Pending marking beside it. On the earliest basses the serial lives here, not on a neck plate.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- GOLD GUARD -->
    <section id="goldguard">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">The Rebuild</span>
        <h2>The Gold Guard Window, 1957 To Mid-1959</h2>
        <p>In the middle of 1957 Fender rebuilt the bass. The single-coil pickup gave way to a hum-cancelling <b>split-coil</b> pickup, the pickguard became <b>gold anodized aluminum</b>, and the headstock took its new larger shape. Under two years later it was over, which is what makes this window collectable.</p>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(PBGG, "1959-fender-precision-bass-anodized-pickguard-scaled.jpg")} alt="Close up of the gold anodized aluminum pickguard on a 1959 Fender Precision Bass" width={1400} />
            <figcaption>The gold anodized aluminum guard. Metal rather than plastic, and the fastest way to place a bass inside the 1957 to mid-1959 window from across a room.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p>The metal guard was not styling. It shielded the electronics against hum and radio interference, which mattered in the era of early tube amplification, and it stayed flat where the single-ply plastic guards of the slab years warped, shrank and cracked.</p>
        <div class="tip">
          <p><b>The wear tells you whether the guard is original.</b> Over decades the gold anodizing wears off where the player's thumb rested, revealing bare silver aluminum underneath. That worn-through patch develops slowly and is very difficult to reproduce convincingly, so it is a point in the guard's favour rather than damage. A gold guard with no wear at all on a heavily played bass is worth a question.</p>
        </div>
        <p>A handful of genuine 1957 basses carry the new split-coil pickup while still wearing the older Telecaster-shaped headstock, which is a factory hybrid rather than a parts assembly. And the neck profile moved inside the window: a soft V in early 1957, carried over from the slab era, becoming a very thick, wide C by 1958, one of the chunkiest necks Fender ever made. Nut width is a generous 1.75 inches throughout.</p>
        <h3 id="gg-end">How The Window Closes</h3>
        <p>Around the middle of 1959 the maple board gave way to a <b>rosewood slab fingerboard</b> and the gold guard gave way to a <b>four ply tortoiseshell celluloid</b> pickguard. Those two changes travel together, and between them they end the gold guard era.</p>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(PBGG, "1959-pbass-rosewood-fretboard.jpg")} alt="A late 1959 rosewood slab fingerboard with clay dot inlays on a Fender Precision Bass" width={1400} />
            <figcaption>The late 1959 rosewood slab board with clay dots. Its arrival is the end of the maple-neck gold-guard bass.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- HEADSTOCK -->
    <section id="headstock">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Pass Two</span>
        <h2>The Logo, And Why Its Colour Matters</h2>
        <p>Three logo eras on this model. The thin <b>spaghetti</b> script runs from the 1950s to 1964. The heavier gold <b>transition</b> logo runs from 1964 to 1967. The bold black <b>CBS</b> logo with its patent text arrives from late 1967 into 1968.</p>
        <div class="tip">
          <p><b>On a Precision Bass the spaghetti logo is silver, not gold.</b> The colour is not consistent across the line and getting it backwards is a common way to misread a headstock: gold on the Stratocaster and the Jazzmaster, silver on the standard Telecaster with gold on the Telecaster Custom, and silver here. <b>The silver carries on through the transition logo too</b>, so on this model the change at 1964 is a change in the weight of the script rather than in its colour: thin and fine, then the same silver drawn much heavier. Colour only re-enters the picture when the black CBS logo arrives from late 1967.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(PBGG, "1959-fender-precision-bass-headstock-scaled.jpg")} alt="The front of the headstock on a 1959 Fender Precision Bass showing the thin spaghetti logo" width={1400} />
            <figcaption>Spaghetti, on a 1959. Thin, fine script, and on this model silver rather than gold.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(PBGG, "1964-fender-precision-bass-transition-logo-scaled.jpg")} alt="The headstock of a 1964 Fender Precision Bass showing the heavier silver transition logo with patent numbers printed below the model name" width={1600} />
            <figcaption>Transition, on a 1964, and still silver. Same colour as the spaghetti above it, drawn much heavier, with the patent numbers set under the model name.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(PBGG, "1971-fender-precision-bass-cbs-logo-scaled.jpg")} alt="The headstock of a 1971 Fender Precision Bass showing the bold black CBS logo with a registered trademark symbol and patent numbers" width={1600} />
            <figcaption>CBS black, on a 1971. The only colour change this headstock ever makes, and the registered trademark symbol beside the script arrives with it.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p class="note-sm">The logo styles across the whole Fender line, and how they date the other models, are on the <a class="spoke" href="/fender-physical-features/#logo">parts and features guide</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- NECK -->
    <section id="neck">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Pass Two</span>
        <h2>The Board, The Dots, And What It Never Got</h2>
        <p>Maple to mid-1959, rosewood after, arriving alongside the tortoiseshell guard. <b>Clay dots run to late 1964</b> and pearloid dots follow, the same window as the rest of the Fender line. Clay reads matte and chalky and ages toward tan or cream, while pearloid is brighter and visibly reflective.</p>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(PBGG, "1959-pbass-rosewood-fretboard.jpg")} alt="Clay dot inlays on the rosewood fingerboard of a 1959 Fender Precision Bass, matte and aged toward tan" width={1400} />
            <figcaption>Clay dots on a 1959 rosewood board. Matte and chalky with no sheen at all, ageing toward tan.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta("fender-jazzmaster-evolution-guide-1958-1971", "fender-jazzmaster-pearloid-dot-inlay-scaled.jpg")} alt="A pearloid dot inlay on a Fender fingerboard, bright white and reflective" width={1600} />
            <figcaption>Pearloid, from late 1964, here on a Jazzmaster board. Bright, white, and reflective, and the same dot went onto the Precision Bass in the same window.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <div class="tip">
          <p><b>The Precision never got binding or block inlays.</b> Not in standard production, at any point in the vintage era. The Jazz Bass took white binding in late 1965 and pearloid blocks in mid-1966, and the Precision simply did not follow it. So an unbound board with dots is correct on a vintage Precision all the way through, and blocks or binding on one need a very good explanation. The confusion is understandable, because Fender's modern seventies Precision reissues are sold with bound, block-inlay necks, but those borrow Jazz Bass styling rather than recreating a vintage Precision.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- PICKUPS -->
    <section id="pickups">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Under The Cover</span>
        <h2>Single Coil To Split Coil</h2>
        <p>The pickup is the second half of the era question, and on this model it is decisive. From 1951 to mid-1957 the Precision carried a <b>single-coil</b> pickup: one bar, sitting under a chrome cover on later examples. From mid-1957 it carried the <b>split-coil</b> pickup, two offset halves wired to cancel hum, which is the design every Precision since has used.</p>
        <p><b>Bottom colour dates the pickup.</b> Fender used black flatwork with cast slug alnico magnets from 1950 to <b>March 1964</b>, then grey flatwork from March 1964 into the late 1970s. It is a quick check on any early bass and it is the same line the Jazz Bass sits on.</p>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(PBGG, "1959-fender-precision-bass-bridge-cover-scaled.jpg")} alt="The chrome ashtray bridge cover installed on a 1959 Fender Precision Bass" width={1400} />
            <figcaption>The chrome ashtray bridge cover. Take it off and turn it over: the foam bonded inside is a factory string mute, not debris somebody left behind.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(PBGG, "1959-fender-precision-bass-pickup-mounting-playe-scaled.jpg")} alt="The gold anodized aluminum pickup mounting plate lifted from the body of a 1959 Fender Precision Bass, with the split coil pickup underneath it" width={1600} />
            <figcaption>The pickup carries its own gold anodized plate, separate from the guard. Lift it and the split coil sits underneath, which is also how you reach the pickup bottoms.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p>Read the rest of the gold-guard hardware as a group, because it is easy to check and hard to fake all at once: a steel bridge plate with four threaded steel saddles, heavy knurled chrome-plated brass flat-top knobs, and a black plastic finger rest or <b>tug bar</b> mounted below the strings for thumb plucking.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- TUNERS -->
    <section id="tuners">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Pass Two</span>
        <h2>Tuners</h2>
        <p>Three eras. <b>Reverse-wind Klusons</b> run from 1958 through early 1966, and they tighten clockwise, which feels backwards to anyone who has only handled modern instruments and is correct here. <b>Non-reverse Klusons</b> follow from early 1966. <b>F-stamped tuners</b> arrive from late 1968 into early 1969.</p>
        <div class="tip">
          <p><b>The F stamp and Schaller are two different things, about seven years apart.</b> Fender produced the F-stamped tuners in house when they arrived at the end of the 1960s. Manufacture only moved to Schaller in Germany by 1976. So an F-stamped tuner on a 1970 bass is not a Schaller part, and treating the stamp as a maker's mark will date a bass wrongly.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(PBGG, "1959-fender-precision-bass-tuner-scaled.jpg")} alt="The back of a reverse-wind nickel plated tuner on a 1959 Fender Precision Bass" width={1400} />
            <figcaption>A reverse-wind tuner on a 1959. Long stem, nickel plated, and it tightens clockwise.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- NECK HEEL -->
    <section id="heel">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Pass Three</span>
        <h2>The Neck Heel Date</h2>
        <p>Take the neck off and the heel carries a date. On the early basses it is written in <b>pencil</b> by the shaper, sometimes with initials. From about 1962 Fender moved to an ink stamp running a model code, then the month, then the year, then a letter for the nut width.</p>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(PB52, "1952-fender-precision-bass-neck-heel-scaled.jpg")} alt="The penciled date on the neck heel of a 1952 Fender Precision Bass" width={1400} />
            <figcaption>A penciled heel date on a 1952. On the early basses this is handwriting, not a stamp, so do not expect a code here.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p>The Precision Bass model code is <b>5</b>. Worth one caution: the neck stamp code table records a second, later code for several models after Fender renumbered at the end of 1965, and it does not record one for the Precision. So read the month and the year and treat the code as a label rather than as a dating tool on this model.</p>
        <p class="note-sm">The stamp format across the whole line, and the full model code table, are on the <a class="spoke" href="/fender-neck-dates/">neck date guide</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- BODY -->
    <section id="body">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Pass Three</span>
        <h2>Body Dates</h2>
        <p>The other place Fender wrote a real date: a pencil date on the body. It corroborates rather than decides, because bodies sat before being matched to necks, so a body date and a neck date a few months apart is the normal result on an untouched bass.</p>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(PB52, "1952-fender-precision-bass-body-date-scaled.jpg")} alt="A penciled body date reading 3-16-53 with the worker's initials above it on an early Fender Precision Bass body" width={1600} />
            <figcaption>Initials and <b>3-16-53</b> in pencil, so this body was prepped in March 1953.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(PBGG, "1959-fender-precision-bass-body-date-scaled.jpg")} alt="A penciled body date on a 1959 Fender Precision Bass body, written in the routing" width={1600} />
            <figcaption>The same pencil on a 1959, ending in <b>59</b>. Written by hand, so expect it to be faint and sometimes half rubbed away.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p>On the slab basses, look at the nail holes and the router marks in the cavities while you are in there, because a respray tends to soften or fill them.</p>
        <p class="note-sm">Where the date hides on every Fender model, and what the pencil, ink and tape markings look like, is on the <a class="spoke" href="/fender-body-dates/">body date guide</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- SERIAL -->
    <section id="serial">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Pass Two, One Vote</span>
        <h2>What The Serial Number Adds</h2>
        <p>A useful vote, and no more. Fender stamped plates in batches and pulled them off the stack in no order, so the number gets you a rough production year and not a month. On this model there is an extra wrinkle: <b>on the earliest basses the serial is not on a neck plate at all</b>, it is stamped into the steel bridge plate.</p>
        <div class="tablewrap"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>Where it is</th><th>Years</th><th>What it looks like</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{plateRows.map((r) => (<tr><td data-label="Where it is"><b>{r[0]}</b></td><td data-label="Years" class="yr">{r[1]}</td><td data-label="What it looks like">{r[2]}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
        </table></div>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(PBGG, "1959-fender-precision-bass-serial-number-scaled.jpg")} alt="The stamped serial number on the neck plate of a 1959 Fender Precision Bass" width={1400} />
            <figcaption>The neck plate serial on a 1959. One vote among several, and four screws away from being somebody else's.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p class="note-sm">There is no Precision Bass serial chart on this page because Fender serials are not model-specific. A 1963 neck plate reads the same whether it ended up under a Precision, a Stratocaster or a Jaguar, so the charts live once on the <a class="spoke" href={HUB_HREF}>full Fender serial number guide</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- SEVENTIES / WHAT IT SKIPPED -->
    <section id="seventies">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Useful Negatives</span>
        <h2>What The Precision Skipped</h2>
        <p>Most dating guides are lists of changes. On this model the changes that <b>did not</b> happen are just as useful, because each one is a red flag when you see it on a bass being sold as an original vintage Precision.</p>
        <p><b>No binding and no block inlays</b>, ever, in standard production. The Jazz Bass took both in 1965 and 1966; the Precision stayed unbound with dots.</p>
        <p><b>No three-bolt neck and no bullet truss rod.</b> The Stratocaster and the Jazz Bass moved to the three-bolt Micro-Tilt joint with a bullet adjuster, the Jazz Bass in late 1974. The Precision kept its <b>four-bolt</b> neck plate and its heel-adjusted truss rod right through the 1970s. So count the bolts: four is correct on a vintage Precision and three is a problem.</p>
        <p>That one runs against the grain. The Precision is usually cast as the plainer of the two basses, the one that got whatever the Jazz Bass got, and on the neck joint it is the Jazz Bass that changed and the Precision that stayed put.</p>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(PBGG, "1959-fender-precision-bass-neck-heel-scaled.jpg")} alt="The neck of a 1959 Fender Precision Bass lifted out of the body, showing the truss rod adjuster screw recessed into the end of the heel" width={1600} />
            <figcaption>The truss rod adjuster, recessed into the end of the heel, here on a 1959. The Precision kept it there through the 1970s, so there is no bullet at the headstock to look for on this model.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- LIMITS -->
    <section id="limits">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Keep It Honest</span>
        <h2>What The Features Can't Prove</h2>
        <p>Every feature on this page dates a part. None of them dates the bass, and with three different instruments wearing this one name, a parts assembly has more places to hide here than on any other Fender.</p>
        <p><b>The gold guard and the slab body are the two features worth faking</b>, because both carry large premiums and neither is difficult to move or to reproduce. A gold anodized guard is a handful of screws. Confirm either against the neck heel date, the body date and the pot codes before you let it set the price.</p>
        <p><b>A correct feature set is not proof of originality.</b> Originality is a whole-instrument judgment that weighs the neck date, the body date, the pots, the pickup, the hardware and the finish against each other and asks whether they tell one story.</p>
        <p><b>Transition dates are approximate, and honest instruments break them.</b> On a Precision that lands hardest on 1952, where slotted and Phillips screws both appear, on 1957, where genuine hybrids exist, and on 1959, where the rosewood and tortoiseshell change happens mid-year.</p>
        <p>The finish is where the most value moves, and the 1958 sunburst is a trap in both directions: a three-tone that has faded to two-tone is normal ageing rather than a refinish, and a suspiciously vivid red on a bass of that age is worth a second look.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- CROSS -->
    <section id="cross">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Put It All Together</span>
        <h2>Confirm It From The Inside</h2>
        <p>The <a class="spoke" href="#heel">neck heel date</a> and the <a class="spoke" href="#body">body date</a> above are the two that give you a month. Two more parts back them up. The <a class="spoke" href="/fender-pot-codes/">pot codes</a> date a potentiometer to the week, which is the tightest number anywhere on the bass, though it is a floor rather than a build date because pots sat in bins. The <a class="spoke" href="/fender-pickup-dates/">pickup dates</a> corroborate when they are present, and on a Precision the bottom colour does real work alongside them. For the parts view across every Fender model, work through <a class="spoke" href="/fender-physical-features/">dating a Fender by its parts</a>.</p>
        <p>For the long version of either end of this model's life, the <a class="spoke" href="/post/1952-fender-precision-bass-guide/">1952 Precision Bass guide</a> covers the slab era in detail and the <a class="spoke" href="/post/gold-guard-fender-precision-bass/">gold guard guide</a> covers 1957 to 1959. The sibling model pages built the same way as this one are the <a class="spoke" href="/fender-stratocaster-dating-guide/">Stratocaster</a>, <a class="spoke" href="/fender-telecaster-dating-guide/">Telecaster</a>, <a class="spoke" href="/fender-jazzmaster-dating-guide/">Jazzmaster</a>, <a class="spoke" href="/fender-jaguar-dating-guide/">Jaguar</a>, <a class="spoke" href="/fender-jazz-bass-dating-guide/">Jazz Bass</a> and <a class="spoke" href="/fender-mustang-dating-guide/">Mustang</a> dating guides.</p>
        <p class="note-sm">This guide is one of the model pages behind the <a class="spoke" href={HUB_HREF}>full Fender serial number guide</a>, where the complete year-by-year serial charts live.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section id="faq" class="pt0">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Expert Questions</span>
        <h2>Common Questions About Dating A Precision Bass</h2>
        <div class="faqs">
          {faqs.map((f) => (
            <details class="faq">
              <summary>{f.q}</summary>
              <div class="faqbody"><p>{f.a}</p></div>
            </details>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- APPRAISAL -->
    <section>
      <div class="measure">
        <div class="appraise" id="appraisal">
          <span class="eyebrow">Free Expert Appraisal</span>
          <h2>Think You Have A Slab Or A Gold Guard?</h2>
          <p>Those are the two that carry the money, and both are worth confirming properly before anybody makes you an offer. Send Joe three clear photos, the front, the back and the headstock, for a free expert appraisal within 24 hours. No obligation, and no screwdriver required.</p>
          <div class="row">
            <a class="call" href={hrefs.tel}>Call Or Text Joe: {contact.phone}</a>
            <a class="send" href="/free-appraisal/">Or Send Photos Online</a>
          </div>
          <p class="note-sm">Thinking about selling? Read how to <a class="spoke" href="/sell-my-fender-guitar/">sell your Fender</a>, or see the <a class="spoke" href="/vintage-fender-stratocaster-value-guide/">Stratocaster</a> and <a class="spoke" href="/vintage-fender-telecaster-value-guide/">Telecaster</a> value guides for a sense of how the vintage market runs.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- BIO -->
    <section class="pt0">
      <div class="measure"><div class="bio" id="about-joe">
        <LicensedImage src={meta("about-me-joe-1.jpg")} alt="Joe Dampt, owner of Joe's Vintage Guitars, holding a vintage guitar" width={150} />
        <div>
          <div class="who"><a href="/about-me/">Joe Dampt</a></div>
          <div class="cred">Owner, Joe's Vintage Guitars · Mesa, AZ · 12 years full-time · 10,000+ appraised · 2,100+ positive reviews</div>
          <p class="biotext">Joe Dampt is the owner of Joe's Vintage Guitars in Mesa, Arizona, where he's spent more than twelve years buying, appraising, and authenticating vintage instruments full-time. He's personally valued over 10,000 guitars for sellers in all 50 states, served as a traveling appraiser for the Treasure Seekers Roadshow, and sourced instruments for Grammy Award-winning artists. Every check on this page comes from that bench work.</p>
        </div>
      </div></div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <!-- Floating TOC (sidebar + mobile FAB + sheet), matches the hub pattern -->
  <aside class="fspot-toc" id="fspot-toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
    <p class="fspot-toc__label">On This Page</p>
    <ul class="fspot-toc__list">
      {tocItems.map(([href, label]) => (<li><a href={href}>{label}</a></li>))}
    </ul>
  </aside>
  <button class="fspot-toc-fab" id="fspot-toc-fab" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="fspot-toc-sheet"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h16"></path></svg>Jump To A Section</button>
  <div class="fspot-toc-sheet" id="fspot-toc-sheet" role="dialog" aria-label="Jump to a section" aria-modal="false">
    <div class="fspot-toc-sheet__head"><span>Jump To A Section</span><button class="fspot-toc-sheet__close" id="fspot-toc-sheet-close" type="button" aria-label="Close section list">&times;</button></div>
    <div class="fspot-toc-sheet__body" id="fspot-toc-sheet-body"></div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .fspot-header-backdrop{position:relative;isolation:isolate;z-index:0;}
  .fspot-header-backdrop__dark{background:var(--color-brand-brown,#3e2a14);height:54px;}
  .fspot-header-backdrop__cream{background:var(--color-brand-brown,#3e2a14);height:56px;border-bottom:6px solid var(--color-brand-brown-tan,#d2b48c);}

  .fspot{
    --paper:#f5efe3; --panel:#ffffff; --panel-2:#f3ead6; --inset:#fffdf8;
    --ink:var(--color-brand-brown,#3e2a14); --ink-strong:var(--color-brand-brown,#3e2a14);
    --muted:var(--color-brand-brown-muted,#7a6a54); --line:#ddd0b6;
    --gold:var(--color-brand-gold,#c8983a); --gold-deep:#8c6a24;
    --rust:var(--color-brand-rust,#a03a1e); --rust-bright:var(--color-brand-rust-bright,#be4b25);
    --amber:#f3e3c2; --amber-line:#e3c88a; --darkbrown:#3a2a18; --darkbrown-2:#4a3520;
    --shadow:0 1px 2px rgba(58,40,23,.06), 0 10px 30px rgba(58,40,23,.08);
    --fd:var(--font-display), "Oswald", system-ui, sans-serif;
    --fm:ui-monospace,"SF Mono",Consolas,"Liberation Mono",monospace;
    --measure:min(92vw,820px);
    background:var(--paper); color:var(--ink); font-size:17px; line-height:1.6;
    padding-bottom:1rem;
  }
  .fspot [id]{scroll-margin-top:90px;}
  .fspot h1,.fspot h2,.fspot h3{font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:600;line-height:1.12;margin:0;color:var(--ink-strong);text-wrap:balance;}
  .fspot h1{font-size:clamp(2rem,4.6vw,2.9rem);letter-spacing:.005em;text-transform:uppercase;}
  .fspot h2{font-size:clamp(1.5rem,3.1vw,2rem);letter-spacing:.01em;}
  .fspot h3{font-size:clamp(1.21rem,2.1vw,1.34rem);letter-spacing:.015em;margin-top:.8rem;color:var(--rust);}
  .fspot p{margin:0;}
  .fspot a{color:var(--gold-deep);text-underline-offset:2px;}
  .fspot a:hover{color:var(--rust);}
  .fspot .measure{width:var(--measure);margin-inline:auto;}
  .fspot section{padding-block:clamp(1.7rem,4vw,2.6rem);}
  .fspot .pt0{padding-top:0;}
  .fspot .rule{height:1px;background:var(--line);width:min(94vw,1080px);margin-inline:auto;}
  .fspot .stack{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:1rem;}
  .fspot .note-sm{font-size:.95rem;color:var(--muted);}
  .fspot .eyebrow{font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:600;font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--rust);display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:.6em;}
  .fspot .eyebrow::before{content:"";width:24px;height:3px;background:var(--gold);}
  .fspot a.spoke{color:var(--gold-deep);text-decoration:none;border-bottom:1.5px dotted var(--gold);font-weight:600;}
  .fspot a.spoke:hover{color:var(--rust);border-bottom-color:var(--rust);}
  .fspot a.back{position:relative;z-index:31;font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:600;font-size:.78rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--rust);text-decoration:none;display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:.4rem;}
  .fspot a.back::before{content:"\2190";font-size:1rem;}
  .fspot a.back:hover{color:var(--rust-bright);}

  .fspot .hero{padding-top:clamp(1.6rem,4vw,2.6rem);padding-bottom:1.4rem;}
  .fspot .lead{font-size:1.16rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.2rem;}
  .fspot .trust{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.4rem .9rem;font-family:var(--fm);font-size:.76rem;color:var(--muted);}
  .fspot .trust span{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:.45rem;}
  .fspot .trust span::before{content:"";width:5px;height:5px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--rust);}

  /* Straight-to-the-hub CTA in the hero, for readers who only want a serial number */
  .fspot .hubcta{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;gap:.8rem 1.1rem;justify-content:space-between;
    background:var(--inset);border:1px solid var(--line);border-left:5px solid var(--gold);border-radius:10px;
    padding:.9rem 1.1rem;box-shadow:var(--shadow);}
  .fspot .hubcta__t{font-size:.95rem;color:var(--muted);flex:1 1 20rem;}
  .fspot .hubcta__t b{color:var(--ink-strong);}
  .fspot .hubcta__b{flex:0 0 auto;font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:600;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.04em;
    font-size:.85rem;color:#fff;background:var(--rust);text-decoration:none;padding:.6rem 1.1rem;border-radius:8px;white-space:nowrap;}
  .fspot .hubcta__b:hover{background:var(--rust-bright);color:#fff;}

  /* The three passes, as three cards rather than three paragraphs */
  .fspot .passes{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(230px,1fr));gap:1rem;}
  .fspot .pass{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:.5rem;background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--line);
    border-top:4px solid var(--rust);border-radius:10px;padding:1rem 1.1rem 1.1rem;box-shadow:var(--shadow);}
  .fspot .pass__n{font-family:var(--fm);font-size:.66rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted);}
  .fspot .pass h3{margin-top:0;font-size:1.15rem;}
  .fspot .pass p{font-size:.95rem;color:var(--muted);}
  .fspot .pass__go{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.35rem;margin-top:auto;padding-top:.5rem;}
  .fspot .pass__go a{font-family:var(--fd);font-size:.78rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.02em;color:var(--gold-deep);
    text-decoration:none;background:var(--panel-2);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:999px;padding:.28rem .7rem;}
  .fspot .pass__go a:hover{color:#fff;background:var(--rust);border-color:var(--rust);}

  /* Row label in the fastest-checks table doubles as a jump link */
  .fspot a.rowjump{font-weight:700;color:var(--ink-strong);text-decoration:none;border-bottom:1.5px dotted var(--gold);}
  .fspot a.rowjump:hover{color:var(--rust);border-bottom-color:var(--rust);}

  /* NOTE: the inline year-table links (a.il) are styled in the is:global block
     BELOW, not here. They are injected with set:html, so they never receive the
     data-astro-cid attribute a scoped rule requires and a scoped rule silently
     does not match them. See CLAUDE.md, "Scoped vs. global CSS for widget HTML". */

  .fspot .secintro{font-size:.97rem;max-width:70ch;color:var(--ink);}
  .fspot .secintro b{color:var(--ink-strong);}

  .fspot .tablewrap{overflow-x:auto;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;box-shadow:var(--shadow);}
  .fspot table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;min-width:520px;background:var(--panel);}
  .fspot .tablewrap.compact table{min-width:0;}
  .fspot th,.fspot td{text-align:left;padding:.6rem 1rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);vertical-align:top;font-size:.94rem;}
  .fspot thead th{font-family:var(--fm);font-size:.65rem;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted);background:var(--panel-2);}
  .fspot tbody tr:last-child td{border-bottom:0;}
  .fspot tbody tr:nth-child(even){background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--panel-2) 45%,transparent);}
  .fspot .code{font-family:var(--fm);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;color:var(--ink);white-space:nowrap;}
  .fspot .yr{font-family:var(--fm);color:var(--rust);font-weight:600;}

  .fspot .figrow{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(230px,1fr));gap:1rem;}
  .fspot .figrow.single{grid-template-columns:minmax(230px,540px);}
  .fspot .figrow.gallery{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:1.4rem;max-width:680px;margin-inline:auto;}
  .fspot figure.sfig{margin:0;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--panel);box-shadow:var(--shadow);}
  .fspot figure.sfig img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;background:var(--panel-2);}
  .fspot figure.sfig figcaption{padding:.55rem .8rem;font-family:var(--fm);font-size:.82rem;color:var(--muted);border-top:1px solid var(--line);}
  .fspot .herofig{margin-top:.3rem;}
  .fspot .herofig figcaption{font-size:.82rem;color:var(--ink);}

  .fspot .tip{background:linear-gradient(135deg,var(--darkbrown-2),var(--darkbrown));color:#f0e1c1;border-radius:10px;padding:1rem 1.15rem;font-size:.95rem;}
  .fspot .tip::before{content:"JOE'S TIP";display:block;font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;color:var(--gold);font-size:.78rem;margin-bottom:.4rem;}
  .fspot .tip b{color:#e0be79;}
  .fspot .warnbox{background:#fbe9e6;border:1px solid #e4a99d;border-left:6px solid var(--rust);border-radius:10px;padding:1rem 1.2rem;box-shadow:var(--shadow);}
  .fspot .warnbox__head{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:.45rem;font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--rust);font-size:.85rem;margin-bottom:.4rem;}
  .fspot .warnbox__head svg{flex:0 0 auto;}
  .fspot .warnbox p{margin:0;color:var(--ink);font-size:.97rem;}

  .fspot .faqs{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:.6rem;}
  .fspot details.faq{border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;background:var(--panel);overflow:hidden;box-shadow:var(--shadow);}
  .fspot details.faq>summary{cursor:pointer;list-style:none;padding:.85rem 1.05rem;font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:600;font-size:1rem;color:var(--ink-strong);display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;gap:.8rem;}
  .fspot details.faq>summary::-webkit-details-marker{display:none;}
  .fspot details.faq>summary::after{content:"+";font-family:var(--fm);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--rust);flex:0 0 auto;}
  .fspot details.faq[open]>summary::after{content:"\2212";}
  .fspot details.faq .faqbody{padding:0 1.05rem 1rem;font-size:.96rem;color:var(--muted);}

  .fspot .appraise{background:var(--amber);border:1px solid var(--amber-line);border-left:5px solid var(--rust);border-radius:12px;padding:clamp(1.4rem,3.5vw,2rem);box-shadow:var(--shadow);}
  .fspot .appraise h2{margin-bottom:.5rem;}
  .fspot .appraise p{color:var(--ink);font-size:1.02rem;}
  .fspot .appraise .row{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.7rem;margin-top:1.1rem;align-items:center;}
  .fspot .appraise .call{font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:700;font-size:1.15rem;letter-spacing:.02em;color:#fff;background:var(--rust);text-decoration:none;padding:.7rem 1.3rem;border-radius:8px;}
  .fspot .appraise .call:hover{background:var(--rust-bright);}
  .fspot .appraise .send{font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:600;text-transform:uppercase;font-size:.86rem;letter-spacing:.04em;color:var(--rust);text-decoration:none;border-bottom:2px solid var(--gold);}

  .fspot .bio{display:flex;gap:1rem;align-items:center;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:12px;padding:1.1rem 1.3rem;background:var(--panel);}
  .fspot .bio img{flex:0 0 auto;width:76px;height:76px;border-radius:50%;object-fit:cover;border:2px solid var(--gold);}
  .fspot .bio .who{font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:600;font-size:1.2rem;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.02em;}
  .fspot .bio .who a{color:var(--ink-strong);text-decoration:none;border-bottom:2px solid var(--gold);}
  .fspot .bio .cred{font-family:var(--fm);font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.2rem;}
  .fspot .bio .biotext{margin-top:.55rem;font-size:.95rem;color:var(--muted);}

  /* mobile card tables */
  @media (max-width:719px){
    .fspot .tablewrap{overflow:visible;border:0;border-radius:0;box-shadow:none;}
    .fspot .tablewrap table{min-width:0;width:100%;display:block;background:transparent;}
    .fspot .tablewrap thead{display:none;}
    .fspot .tablewrap tbody{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:.65rem;}
    .fspot .tablewrap tr{display:block;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;background:var(--panel);padding:.7rem .85rem;box-shadow:var(--shadow);}
    .fspot .tablewrap tbody tr:nth-child(even){background:var(--panel);}
    .fspot .tablewrap td{display:block;border:0;padding:.25rem 0;font-size:.95rem;}
    .fspot .tablewrap td::before{content:attr(data-label);display:block;font-family:var(--fm);font-size:.62rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted);margin-bottom:.1rem;}
    .fspot .tablewrap .code,.fspot .tablewrap .yr{white-space:normal;}
  }

  /* floating TOC */
  .fspot-toc{display:none;z-index:25;background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--color-brand-brown-tan,#d2b48c);border-radius:10px;width:260px;max-height:calc(100vh - 160px);padding:16px 18px;position:fixed;top:120px;right:24px;overflow-y:auto;box-shadow:0 2px 12px rgba(0,0,0,.08);}
  @media (min-width:1440px){.fspot-toc{display:block;}}
  .fspot-toc__label{font-family:var(--font-display),sans-serif;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.13em;color:var(--color-brand-brown,#3e2a14);border-bottom:1px solid #a03a1e2e;margin:0 0 10px;padding-bottom:8px;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;}
  .fspot-toc__list{margin:0;padding:0;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.4;list-style:none;}
  .fspot-toc__list li{margin:0;}
  .fspot-toc__list a{color:var(--color-brand-brown-muted,#6c5238);border-radius:4px;padding:5px 6px;text-decoration:none;display:block;}
  .fspot-toc__list a:hover{background:var(--color-brand-cream-warm,#f5ead4);color:var(--color-brand-rust,#a03a1e);}
  .fspot-toc-fab{display:none;}
  .fspot-toc-sheet{display:none;}
  @media (max-width:1439.98px){
    .fspot-toc-fab{z-index:45;background:var(--color-brand-rust-bright,#be4b25);color:#fff;font-family:var(--font-display),sans-serif;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase;cursor:pointer;border:none;border-radius:999px;align-items:center;gap:8px;padding:11px 18px;font-size:13px;font-weight:600;display:inline-flex;position:fixed;bottom:1.5rem;left:16px;box-shadow:0 4px 16px rgba(0,0,0,.28);}
    .fspot-toc-fab svg{width:18px;height:18px;}
    .fspot-toc-fab[aria-expanded="true"]{background:var(--color-brand-rust-dark,#682412);}
    .fspot-toc-sheet{z-index:46;opacity:0;visibility:hidden;background:#fff;border:1px solid #a03a1e33;border-radius:12px;flex-direction:column;width:min(340px,100vw - 32px);max-height:60vh;transition:opacity .2s,transform .2s,visibility .2s;display:flex;position:fixed;bottom:5rem;left:16px;overflow-y:auto;transform:translateY(12px);box-shadow:0 10px 34px rgba(0,0,0,.28);}
    .fspot-toc-sheet.fspot-toc-sheet--open{opacity:1;visibility:visible;transform:translateY(0);}
  }
  .fspot-toc-sheet__head{font-family:var(--font-display),sans-serif;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-brand-rust-dark,#682412);background:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #a03a1e26;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;padding:12px 16px;font-size:13px;font-weight:700;display:flex;position:sticky;top:0;}
  .fspot-toc-sheet__close{color:var(--color-brand-brown-muted,#6c5238);cursor:pointer;background:none;border:none;padding:0 4px;font-size:24px;line-height:1;}
  .fspot-toc-sheet__body{padding:8px 10px;}
  .fspot-toc-sheet__body .fspot-toc__list{font-size:14px;}
  .fspot-toc-sheet__body .fspot-toc__list a{padding:8px 6px;}
</style>

<!--
  GLOBAL, and it has to be. The inline jump links in the year table are injected
  with set:html, so Astro never stamps them with the data-astro-cid attribute
  that a scoped rule compiles into its selector. A scoped rule here matches
  nothing and fails silently: the links render, they just look like plain text.
  Both selectors stay prefixed with .fspot so nothing leaks off this page.
-->
<style is:global>
  .fspot a.il{color:var(--gold-deep,#8c6a24);text-decoration:none;border-bottom:1px dotted var(--gold,#c8983a);font-weight:600;}
  .fspot a.il:hover{color:var(--rust,#a03a1e);border-bottom-color:var(--rust,#a03a1e);border-bottom-style:solid;}
</style>

<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    // Floating TOC: clone list into the mobile sheet, wire the FAB
    var list = document.querySelector('#fspot-toc .fspot-toc__list');
    var body = document.getElementById('fspot-toc-sheet-body');
    if (list && body) body.appendChild(list.cloneNode(true));
    var fab = document.getElementById('fspot-toc-fab'),
        sheet = document.getElementById('fspot-toc-sheet'),
        closeBtn = document.getElementById('fspot-toc-sheet-close');
    function setOpen(o) { if (!sheet) return; sheet.classList.toggle('fspot-toc-sheet--open', o); if (fab) fab.setAttribute('aria-expanded', o ? 'true' : 'false'); }
    if (fab) fab.addEventListener('click', function () { setOpen(!sheet.classList.contains('fspot-toc-sheet--open')); });
    if (closeBtn) closeBtn.addEventListener('click', function () { setOpen(false); });
    document.addEventListener('click', function (e) { if (sheet && fab && !sheet.contains(e.target) && !fab.contains(e.target)) setOpen(false); });
    document.addEventListener('keydown', function (e) { if (e.key === 'Escape') setOpen(false); });
    if (sheet) sheet.addEventListener('click', function (e) { if (e.target.closest('a')) setOpen(false); });
  })();
</script>
